ions are $y=0$ and $y=3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the intervals of $y$ for which $f(y)>0$ and $f(y)<0.$ \n\nConstructing a sign table, we get the following:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $(0,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-{y}^{2}$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $y-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,3),$ and negative for $y\\in (3,\\infty ).$\n\n**Step 3**: Draw the phase line using the following recipe:\n\n- Draw a vertical $y$-axis\n\n- Mark the equilibrium solutions\n\n- Draw arrows pointing up in the intervals where $f(y)>0$\n\n- Draw arrows pointing down in the intervals where $f(y)<0$\n\nApplying the above recipe, we get the following phase line for our differential equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/1de4442141a2555ebb9d7c9d4e29e0555566b5177bcd4166e59331459b8621aa.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-98558/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66924d52-fd39-5308-b78e-611e3d3129ad"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-98558"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-98527/selection/59a31c38851ad53ff959ce8e56aa22a2d502ccc6a7ef8069f0ce1b6a0e58a7cf" :answer/id #uuid "24851259-9065-5ec4-b0b0-f12cca0d497c" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/7f44aa29a8d99f144707d3126102ad633fd8b0f947ae16d862870444a5125793.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98527/selection/0511a72a73105597f01f12c8a432dd28b3087a7aa73662196189c740a3366fc7" :answer/id #uuid "e55e872c-d5ab-57cc-a51b-ca5204f0c597" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/8d7a4b4ac510e040a6d6c