answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98526/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "c4cd3b8c-0784-5b5a-8b66-21a478f8d1c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-98526/selection/14681d6d3d5b077d711fd486bab2f6112adc9d3c7d8210b71440b8d0f646f2db" :answer-field/choices ["q-98526/selection/4096c108831978edd2f5f94b67f2b64b0b394cc08b2d8f805fe2de7d460c2e54" "q-98526/selection/faaabc7fef26eec7f177f5aae6dd4e0beca58ae63aa3b072626fcaa184568d63" "q-98526/selection/d999f1988710fbf23a6e389c8a3b846312df0bf09585cb87ae80fca4ea975a0d" "q-98526/selection/f02c45ca498bbcdcb12126f4455b5cd5e1afec3f5cea71ed36a614b4446e7c47" "q-98526/selection/14681d6d3d5b077d711fd486bab2f6112adc9d3c7d8210b71440b8d0f646f2db"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-98526" :question/id #uuid "06ad98a7-533f-59fe-919c-9f9dc27f7c18" :question/math-academy-id "q-98526" :question/problem "Which of the following is the phase line of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-{y}^{2}+10y-24?$" :question/worked-solution "To draw the phase line of the differential equation, we proceed using the following three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation. \n\nIn this case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{y}^{2}+10y-24 & =0 \\\\ -({y}^{2}-10y+24) & =0 \\\\ -(y-4)(y-6) & =0 \\\\ y & =4,6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium solutions are $y=4$ and $y=6.$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the intervals of $y$ for which $f(y)>0$ and $f(y)<0.$ \n\nConstructing a sign table, we get the following:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,4)$ | $(4,6)$ | $(6,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-(y-4)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $y-6$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (4,6),$ and negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,4)\\cup (6,\\infty ).$\n\n**Step 3**: Draw the phase line using the following recipe:\n\n- Draw a vertical $y$-axis\n\n- Mark the equilibrium solutions\n\n- Draw arrows pointing up in the intervals where $f(y)>0$\n\n- Draw arrows pointing down in the intervals where $f(y)<0$\n\nApplying the above recipe, we get the following phase line for our differential equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/b123be00d466cf117b6c3f19376230e240a6ef0a64677b5d18457d43d736fd94.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-98526/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66924d52-fd39-5308-b78e-611e3d3129ad"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-98526"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-98646/selection/421ba3cf61c86c7d854403490925f53920cd3c015c12342698571d9a74aca2fd" :answer/id #uuid "95f56b8d-796a-5562-8393-f9d46e997cdf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98646/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "7b8d8d0f-9939-54c2-aa85-c59add9750fc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98646/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "baa80c54-0a21-5c23-b55b-212d2a98d13b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98646/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "1430de37-6fec-5609-b7ba-36028c5541d9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98646/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "2204042e-ba29-54de-a806-c9c98d4df02f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98646/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "46f85cd1-c981-5424-bb07-e79adc1aa5cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-98646/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer-field/choices ["q-98646/selection/421ba3cf61c86c7d854403490925f53920cd3c015c12342698571d9a74aca2fd" "q-98646/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-98646/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-98646/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-98646/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-98646" :question/id #uuid "d288e73b-370f-5ae4-a77c-c83deeee0c8f" :question/math-academy-id "q-98646" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/54cfcbaa5ce20c1881f35e83bb0ec28fa1399cc5100ee745941ff7bb52ac7705.png)\n\nConsider the phase line shown above. Which of the following differential equations could have this phase line?\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-(y+2)^{2}(y-1)$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(y+2)(y-1)$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-(y+2)(y-1)^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that all three differential equations are of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThe phase line gives us the following information:\n\n- The equilibrium solutions are $y=-2$ and $y=1.$\n\n- $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2).$\n\n- $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-2,1)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's study each of the given equations.\n\n- We start by considering ${y}^{′}=-(y+2)^{2}(y-1).$\n\nFirst, $f(y)=-(y+2)^{2}(y-1),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-2,1.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nHowever, we note that $f(y)>0$ for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (-2,1).$ This does *not* correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation I does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Next, we consider ${y}^{′}=(y+2)(y-1).$\n\nFirst, $f(y)=(y+2)(y-1),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-2,1.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nHowever, we note that $f(y)>0$ for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$ This does *not* correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation II does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Finally, we consider ${y}^{′}=-(y+2)(y-1)^{2}.$\n\nFirst, we see that $f(y)=-(y+2)(y-1)^{2},$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-2,1.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nNow, we can construct a sign table for $f(y)$ as follows: \n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,1)$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-(y+2)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $(y-1)^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-2,1)\\cup (1,\\infty ),$ and $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2).$ This corresponds to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation III has the given phase line.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-98646/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e71c0c9-7dd1-5f09-a55c-96b5bed1ee9a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-98646"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-100569/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "607ed537-0e43-5503-b7c6-79a2337febbd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-100569/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "8c11f0c0-3110-515c-ae31-c48f989c42d4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-100569/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "c3c8670b-5695-5682-8d1e-e93412e4d882" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-100569/selection/c5361bc7e0ee1fc99d41021ab98e1ca699e408307178f1c31af6bc0071e78ea0" :answer/id #uuid "8b2eaeef-cade-599b-80f5-0f383e9a5007" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-100569/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "a6050415-1d2c-5b54-ab42-a6c1cc271d4a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-100569/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "1f9fbbcb-fed6-5dfd-b12f-79b853e0c472" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-100569/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer-field/choices ["q-100569/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-100569/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-100569/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-100569/selection/c5361bc7e0ee1fc99d41021ab98e1ca699e408307178f1c31af6bc0071e78ea0" "q-100569/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-100569" :question/id #uuid "baceb21e-e41f-5237-bc5c-3f49cd7cc51a" :question/math-academy-id "q-100569" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/375647b4e2527841b48da5405effe2f15502f1d972586556796a55bcff441b94.png)\n\nConsider the phase line shown above. Which of the following differential equations could have this phase line?\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-y(y+1)^{2}$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{3}-y$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-y(y+1)(y-2)$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that all three differential equations are of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThe phase line gives us the following information:\n\n- The equilibrium solutions are $y=-1,$ $y=0$ and $y=2.$\n\n- $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (0,2).$\n\n- $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-1,0)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's study each of the given equations.\n\n- We start by considering ${y}^{′}=-y(y+1)^{2}.$\n\nIn this case, $f(y)=-y(y+1)^{2},$ and the equilibrium solutions are $y=-1,0$ only. \n\nTherefore, equation I does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Next, we consider ${y}^{′}={y}^{3}-y.$\n\nIn this case, $f(y)=y(y-1)(y+1),$ and the equilibrium solutions are $y=-1,0,1$ only. \n\nTherefore, equation II does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Finally, we consider ${y}^{′}=-y(y+1)(y-2).$\n\nFirst, we see that $f(y)=-y(y+1)(y-2),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-1,0,2.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nNow, we can construct a sign table for $f(y)$ as follows: \n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $(-1,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+1$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $-y$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $y-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-1,0)\\cup (2,\\infty ),$ and $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (0,2).$ This corresponds to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation III has the given phase line.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-100569/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e71c0c9-7dd1-5f09-a55c-96b5bed1ee9a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-100569"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-335824/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "fef71e81-677d-