se_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/96df34b3f844ee5abace182769011a3b4b2e413100fbca4cf25e829ca67948cc.png)\n\nConsider the phase line shown above. Which of the following differential equations could have this phase line?\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(y-2)^{2}(y-4)$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(y-2)(y-4)(y-6)$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(y-2)^{2}(y-4)(y-6)$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that all three differential equations are of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThe phase line gives us the following information:\n\n- The equilibrium solutions are $y=2,$ $y=4$ and $y=6.$\n\n- $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,2)\\cup (2,4)\\cup (6,\\infty ).$\n\n- $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (4,6).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's study each of the given equations.\n\n- We start by considering\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(y-2)^{2}(y-4).\n$$\n\nIn this case, $f(y)=(y-2)^{2}(y-4),$ and the equilibrium solutions are $y=2,4$ only.\n\nTherefore, equation I does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Next, we consider\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(y-2)(y-4)(y-6).\n$$\n\nIn this case, $f(y)=(y-2)(y-4)(y-6),$ and the equilibrium solutions are $y=2,4,6.$\n\nHowever, we note that $f(y)<0$ for $y\\in (-\\infty ,2).$ This does not correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation II does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Finally, we consider ${y}^{′}=(y-2)^{2}(y-4)(y-6).$\n\nFirst, we see that $f(y)=(y-2)^{2}(y-4)(y-6),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=2,4,6.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nNow, we can construct a sign table for $f(y)$ as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,2)$ | $(2,4)$ | $(4,6)$ | $(6,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $(y-2)^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-4$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-6$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (4,6),$ and $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,2)\\cup (2,4)\\cup (6,\\infty ).$\nTherefore, equation III has the given phase line.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-335824/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e71c0c9-7dd1-5f09-a55c-96b5bed1ee9a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-335824"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-98643/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "0dda8671-52b1-53eb-ab1b-0f9c50326a67" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98643/selection/421ba3cf61c86c7d854403490925f53920cd3c015c12342698571d9a74aca2fd" :answer/id #uuid "26986fa5-9fe6-53ea-b7d8-7162fe332e28" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98643/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "12a29b97-af35-541a-83a0-a26b96ed2f00" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98643/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "5fa42dce-003e-5f69-b01c-aaaca30ee462" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98643/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "248e9fb2-0278-5d3f-bde0-b6314b89d0a6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98643/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "e8bf5d8f-985a-58c1-ac95-46d283e15e25" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-98643/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer-field/choices ["q-98643/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-98643/selection/421ba3cf61c86c7d854403490925f53920cd3c015c12342698571d9a74aca2fd" "q-98643/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-98643/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-98643/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-98643" :question/id #uuid "d698ab4a-533d-5805-b371-ab030fdf00d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-98643" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/96740fdcc0dc6aa22fa097ba714669fae4ef79543aafad72b3fc3db7722f83d1.png)\n\nConsider the phase line shown above. Which of the following differential equations could have this phase line?\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-y(y-2)$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-y(y-2)^{2}$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}+2y$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that all three differential equations are of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThe phase line gives us the following information:\n\n- The equilibrium solutions are $y=0$ and $y=2.$\n\n- $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,0).$\n\n- $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (0,2)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's study each of the given equations.\n\n- We start by considering ${y}^{′}=-y(y-2).$\n\nFirst, $f(y)=-y(y-2),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=0,2.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nHowever, we note that $f(y)>0$ for $y\\in (0,2).$ This does *not* correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation I does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Next, we consider ${y}^{′}=-y(y-2)^{2}.$\n\nFirst, we see that $f(y)=-y(y-2)^{2},$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=0,2.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nNow, we can construct a sign table for $f(y)$ as follows: \n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-y$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $(y-2)^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n\n \nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for  $y\\in (-\\infty ,0),$ and $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (0,2)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$ This corresponds to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation II has the given phase line.\n\n- Finally, we consider ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}+2y.$\n\nIn this case, $f(y)=y(y+2),$ and the equilibrium solution are $y=-2,0.$ \n\nTherefore, equation III does not have the given phase line.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-98643/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e71c0c9-7dd1-5f09-a55c-96b5bed1ee9a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-98643"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-98636/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "e2a49b1e-c1c3-5053-abdc-f4f1198955a4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98636/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "dd43307c-5510-58b9-a175-23eae1335632" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98636/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "653340e9-73be-57cc-b860-01b9f214c5be" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98636/selection/421ba3cf61c86c7d854403490925f53920cd3c015c12342698571d9a74aca2fd" :answer/id #uuid "80c40349-53f1-5999-bd42-a070b7950fb6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98636/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "900cb579-beb3-599d-a0ef-125010a02ee3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98636/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "3dde0b73-8f23-5261-9223-afac905fa2ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-98636/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer-field/choices ["q-98636/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-98636/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-98636/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-98636/selection/421ba3cf61c86c7d854403490925f53920cd3c015c12342698571d9a74aca2fd" "q-98636/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-98636" :question/id #uuid "4a946ccf-7250-5f83-ba8c-44f2d4585333" :question/math-academy-id "q-98636" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/b5afe944331145c5253b2f1100f642b8a91a538295f3c84877eeb86870a04915.png)\n\nConsider the phase line shown above. Which of the following differential equations could have this phase line?\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y({y}^{2}-4)$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y(4-{y}^{2})$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}-4$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that all three differential equations are of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThe phase line gives us the following information:\n\n- The equilibrium solutions are $y=-2,$ $y=0$ and $y=2.$\n\n- $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-2,0)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$\n\n- $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (0,2).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's study each of the given equations.\n\n- We start by considering ${y}^{′}=y({y}^{2}-4).$\n\nFirst, we see that $f(y)=y(y+2)(y-2),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-2,0,2.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nNow, we can construct a sign table for $f(y)$ as follows: \n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (0,2),$ and $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-2,0)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$ This corresponds to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation I has the given phase line.\n\n- Next, we consider ${y}^{′}=y(4-{y}^{2}).$\n\nFirst, $f(y)=y(4-{y}^{2}),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-2,0,2.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nHowever, we note that $f(y)>0$ for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2).$ This does *not* correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation II does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Finally, we consider ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}-4.$\n\nIn this case, $f(y)={y}^{2}-4,$ and the equilibrium solutions are $y=\\pm 2$ only.\n\nTherefore, equation III does not have the given phase line.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-98636/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e71c0c9-7dd1-5f09-a55c-96b5bed1ee9a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-98636"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-97931"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/ab2ec609e7add6dc3a44fc2f70fad4d4da325daf5614d80941ad499254962e14.png)\n\nConsider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the phase line corresponding to this differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "From the graph of the function $f(y),$ we can deduce the following information:\n\n- The solutions to $f(y)=0$ are $y=-1$ and $y=3.$ Therefore $y=-1,3$ are the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation.\n\n- $f(y)>0$ when $y\\in (-1,3).$\n\n- $f(y)<0$ when $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (3,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, we can draw the phase line using the following recipe:\n\n- Draw a vertical $y$-axis\n\n- Mark the equilibrium solutions\n\n- Draw arrows pointing up in the intervals where $f(y)>0$\n\n- Draw arrows pointing down in the intervals where $f(y)<0$\n\nApplying the above recipe, we get the following phase line for our differential equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/8073b3575bd48710fe7744221dbebe7f9d0656991ea4a9bea8e11e06431f63d7.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-97917/selection/bc73bf7a4de794940db4f5b827e77047412688364e0562e893444c16d26d04be" :answer/id #uuid "01111f29-7899-5ce6-92f0-42206a38c570" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/b24f89fa181f60b0b2d6fdf1800339e1c88a2eb8da3d1ed87e0cca34140e2479.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97917/selection/33f5b14a14c421c30dea241414173a2b0b853a53b5d669e744df315429ae53a2" :answer/id #uuid "7f385aab-2bb7-5b71-af9b-efdb1a6e34f3" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/5fa18a0300f34e5a471dac55a30a1edc838717ef154f3f776f6fabe08536a3e6.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97917/selection/71a91b2f0f84acc4204d142b0a7428750fdacb1c8d5384eb98b8931e6cdc2ff5" :answer/id #uuid "5a79aafe-faa3-508a-9358-c8a5afb65bb7" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academ